var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#contributing","page":"Contributing guidelines","text":"First of all, thanks for the interest!\n\nWe welcome all kinds of contribution, including, but not limited to code, documentation, examples, configuration, issue creating, etc.\n\nBe polite and respectful, and follow the code of conduct.","title":"Contributing guidelines"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Bug-reports-and-discussions","page":"Contributing guidelines","text":"If you think you found a bug, feel free to open an issue. Focused suggestions and requests can also be opened as issues. Before opening a pull request, start an issue or a discussion on the topic, please.","title":"Bug reports and discussions"},{"category":"section","location":"contributing/#Working-on-an-issue","page":"Contributing guidelines","text":"If you found an issue that interests you, comment on that issue what your plans are. If the solution to the issue is clear, you can immediately create a pull request (see below). Otherwise, say what your proposed solution is and wait for a discussion around it.\n\ntip: Tip\nFeel free to ping us after a few days if there are no responses.\n\nIf your solution involves code (or something that requires running the package locally), check the developer documentation. Otherwise, you can use the GitHub interface directly to create your pull request.","title":"Working on an issue"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#dev_docs","page":"Developer docs","text":"note: Contributing guidelines\nIf you haven't, please read the Contributing guidelines first.\n\nIf you want to make contributions to this package that involves code, then this guide is for you.","title":"Developer documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#First-time-clone","page":"Developer docs","text":"tip: If you have writing rights\nIf you have writing rights, you don't have to fork. Instead, simply clone and skip ahead. Whenever upstream is mentioned, use origin instead.\n\nIf this is the first time you work with this repository, follow the instructions below to clone the repository.\n\nFork this repo\nClone your repo (this will create a git remote called origin)\nAdd this repo as a remote:\ngit remote add upstream https://github.com/langestefan/BiTemporalData.jl\n\nThis will ensure that you have two remotes in your git: origin and upstream. You will create branches and push to origin, and you will fetch and update your local main branch from upstream.","title":"First time clone"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Linting-and-formatting","page":"Developer docs","text":"Install a plugin on your editor to use EditorConfig. This will ensure that your editor is configured with important formatting settings.\n\nWe use prek (a fast, drop-in pre-commit reimplementation) to run the linters and formatters, both locally and in CI. The Julia code is formatted using Runic.jl; prek provisions Runic itself, but the ExplicitImports check runs from your global environment, so install it there first:\n\njulia> # Press ]\npkg> activate\npkg> add ExplicitImports\n\nTo install prek, use its standalone installer (no Python required):\n\n# macOS / Linux\ncurl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -LsSf https://github.com/j178/prek/releases/latest/download/prek-installer.sh | sh\n\n# Windows\npowershell -ExecutionPolicy ByPass -c \"irm https://github.com/j178/prek/releases/latest/download/prek-installer.ps1 | iex\"\n\nOr via a package manager: brew install prek, cargo install --locked prek, or mise use prek. See the prek README for details.\n\nWith prek installed, activate it as a git pre-commit hook:\n\nprek install\n\nTo run the linting and formatting manually, enter the command below:\n\nprek run -a\n\nNow, you can only commit if all the prek checks pass.","title":"Linting and formatting"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Link-checking-locally","page":"Developer docs","text":"We use lychee for link checking in CI. You can run it locally to avoid waiting for CI. First, install lychee, then run against the repository root using the project config:\n\nlychee --no-progress --config lychee.toml .","title":"Link checking locally"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Testing","page":"Developer docs","text":"As with most Julia packages, you can just open Julia in the repository folder, activate the environment, and run test:\n\njulia> # press ]\npkg> activate .\npkg> test","title":"Testing"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Working-on-a-new-issue","page":"Developer docs","text":"We try to keep a linear history in this repo, so it is important to keep your branches up-to-date.\n\nFetch from the remote and fast-forward your local main\ngit fetch upstream\ngit switch main\ngit merge --ff-only upstream/main\nBranch from main to address the issue (see below for naming)\ngit switch -c 42-add-answer-universe\nPush the new local branch to your personal remote repository\ngit push -u origin 42-add-answer-universe\nCreate a pull request to merge your remote branch into the org main.","title":"Working on a new issue"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Branch-naming","page":"Developer docs","text":"If there is an associated issue, add the issue number.\nIf there is no associated issue, and the changes are small, add a prefix such as \"typo\", \"hotfix\", \"small-refactor\", according to the type of update.\nIf the changes are not small and there is no associated issue, then create the issue first, so we can properly discuss the changes.\nUse dash separated imperative wording related to the issue (e.g., 14-add-tests, 15-fix-model, 16-remove-obsolete-files).","title":"Branch naming"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Commit-message","page":"Developer docs","text":"Use imperative or present tense, for instance: Add feature or Fix bug.\nHave informative titles.\nWhen necessary, add a body with details.\nIf there are breaking changes, add the information to the commit message.","title":"Commit message"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Before-creating-a-pull-request","page":"Developer docs","text":"tip: Atomic git commits\nTry to create \"atomic git commits\" (recommended reading: The Utopic Git History).\n\nMake sure the tests pass.\nMake sure the pre-commit tests pass.\nFetch any main updates from upstream and rebase your branch, if necessary:\ngit fetch upstream\ngit rebase upstream/main BRANCH_NAME\nThen you can open a pull request and work with the reviewer to address any issues.","title":"Before creating a pull request"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Building-and-viewing-the-documentation-locally","page":"Developer docs","text":"Following the latest suggestions, we recommend using LiveServer to build the documentation. Here is how you do it:\n\nRun julia --project=docs to open Julia in the environment of the docs.\nIf this is the first time building the docs\nPress ] to enter pkg mode\nRun pkg> dev . to use the development version of your package\nPress backspace to leave pkg mode\nRun julia> using LiveServer\nRun julia> servedocs()","title":"Building and viewing the documentation locally"},{"category":"section","location":"developer/#Making-a-new-release","page":"Developer docs","text":"To create a new release, you can follow these simple steps:\n\nCreate a branch release-x.y.z\nUpdate version in Project.toml\nUpdate the CHANGELOG.md:\nRename the section \"Unreleased\" to \"[x.y.z] - yyyy-mm-dd\" (i.e., version under brackets, dash, and date in ISO format)\nAdd a new section on top of it named \"Unreleased\"\nAdd a new link in the bottom for version \"x.y.z\"\nChange the \"[unreleased]\" link to use the latest version - end of line, vx.y.z ... HEAD.\nCreate a commit \"Release vx.y.z\", push, create a PR, wait for it to pass, merge the PR.\nGo back to main screen and click on the latest commit (link: https://github.com/langestefan/BiTemporalData.jl/commit/main)\nAt the bottom, write @JuliaRegistrator register\n\nAfter that, you only need to wait and verify:\n\nWait for the bot to comment (should take < 1m) with a link to a PR to the registry\nFollow the link and wait for a comment on the auto-merge\nThe comment should said all is well and auto-merge should occur shortly\nAfter the merge happens, TagBot will trigger and create a new GitHub tag. Check on https://github.com/langestefan/BiTemporalData.jl/releases\nAfter the release is create, a \"docs\" GitHub action will start for the tag.\nAfter it passes, a deploy action will run.\nAfter that runs, the stable docs should be updated. Check them and look for the version number.","title":"Making a new release"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/bitemporal-data-model/#The-bitemporal-data-model","page":"The bitemporal data model","text":"This guide explains the model BiTemporalData.jl is built on: two independent time axes, and how they let you answer \"what did we believe was true, and when?\"","title":"The bitemporal data model"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/bitemporal-data-model/#Two-kinds-of-time","page":"The bitemporal data model","text":"Every fact is tracked along two axes:\n\nValid time — when the fact is true in the world.\nTransaction time — when the system believed it.\n\nThese are genuinely different. Valid time is a property of the world (a price holds from a date, a sensor reads at an instant). Transaction time is a property of the database (a row was written, then later corrected). Keeping them apart is the whole point.","title":"Two kinds of time"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/bitemporal-data-model/#Why-two-axes","page":"The bitemporal data model","text":"One axis can't tell two different kinds of change apart:\n\nThe world changed. Yesterday's price was right; today there is a new one.\nWe changed our mind. Yesterday's price was wrong; we now know the correct value for that same day.\n\nWith a single \"last updated\" timestamp both look identical — the old number is gone either way. Bitemporal storage records both axes, so you can reproduce exactly what the system believed at any past moment, and separate a real-world change from a correction.","title":"Why two axes"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/bitemporal-data-model/#A-record-is-a-rectangle","page":"The bitemporal data model","text":"A stored Record covers a half-open box: [valid_from, valid_to) on the valid-time axis and [tx_from, tx_to) on the transaction-time axis. Writes are append-only — the only field that ever changes is tx_to, which is closed when a later write supersedes the record. A record whose tx_to is still open (MAX_DT) is currently believed.\n\nA query picks one point on each axis (valid_at, tx_at) and returns the record whose box contains that point.","title":"A record is a rectangle"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/bitemporal-data-model/#The-three-writes","page":"The bitemporal data model","text":"insert! — record a new fact over a valid range.\ncorrect! — \"we were wrong\": supersede a value. The old record is closed in transaction time, not deleted, so earlier beliefs stay reproducible.\namend! — \"the world changed on a date\": split the valid-time timeline, keeping the old value before the change and the new value after.\n\ncorrect! moves along the transaction axis; amend! moves along the valid axis. That distinction is the model in one sentence.","title":"The three writes"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/bitemporal-data-model/#Reading-the-store","page":"The bitemporal data model","text":"as_of answers a single (valid_at, tx_at) point.\nsnapshot freezes the whole store at one tx_at — the reproducible, leakage-proof read boundary for analytics and ML.","title":"Reading the store"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/bitemporal-data-model/#Where-to-next","page":"The bitemporal data model","text":"The Reference lists every operation and type.\nThe package README has a runnable quick-start walking through insert!, correct!, amend!, and snapshot.","title":"Where to next"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Index","page":"Reference","text":"Pages = [\"reference.md\"]","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Operations","page":"Reference","text":"insert! and diff extend Base functions, so they are documented explicitly here; the remaining operations and types are listed below.","title":"Operations"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Base.insert!-Tuple{BitemporalStore, Any, Any}","page":"Reference","text":"insert!(s, key, value; effective_from, effective_to = MAX_DT, asserted_at = now(UTC), check_overlap = false)\n\nRecord a new fact over [effective_from, effective_to). effective_from/effective_to accept any TimeType (a Date is taken as midnight). Returns the stored Record.\n\nBy default insert! does not check for overlap with an existing asserted record: two overlapping current assertions both stand, and a read resolves them by the as_of tie rule (latest assertive_from, then the later write). To re-state a fact, use correct!, which closes the prior assertion first. Pass check_overlap = true to instead reject an insert! that overlaps an asserted record (it points you at correct!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.insert!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Base.diff-Tuple{BitemporalStore}","page":"Reference","text":"diff(s; assertive_at_old, assertive_at_new) -> NamedTuple of column vectors\n\nRecords whose currently-asserted value changed between two assertive times. Columns entity, effective_from, effective_to, old_value, new_value, kind, where kind is one of :inserted (present only at assertive_at_new), :retracted (present only at assertive_at_old), or :corrected (same (entity, effective_from, effective_to), different value). Unchanged rows are omitted, so the result is empty iff nothing the store believes changed. Extends Base.diff. Tables.jl-compatible.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.diff"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.MAX_DT","page":"Reference","text":"Sentinel for an open range end: typemax(DateTime). assertive_to == MAX_DT means currently asserted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.MAX_DT"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.BitemporalStore","page":"Reference","text":"BitemporalStore{K,V}\n\nStore of V values keyed by K. Backends implement get_records, put_record!, close_tx!, entities and inherit insert!, correct!, amend!, as_of, history, snapshot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.BitemporalStore"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.ColumnarStore","page":"Reference","text":"ColumnarStore{K,V}()\n\nIn-memory struct-of-arrays backend: every record field is a column in one contiguous vector, with a per-key index of row positions. Same semantics as MemoryStore, but laid out so that snapshot is a single linear pass that builds the value column directly (no per-record objects, no deserialization).\n\nThis is the read path for ML / bulk-analytics workloads: a snapshot's value column comes out as a contiguous Vector{V}, ready to hand to a model or copy to a device in one shot. Not thread-safe; wrap in ThreadSafe for concurrent use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.ColumnarStore"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.DuckDBStore","page":"Reference","text":"DuckDBStore{K,V}(path::AbstractString; table = \"records\")\nDuckDBStore{K,V}(db::DuckDB.DB; table = \"records\")\n\nDuckDB-backed bitemporal store. Requires the DuckDB extension: run using DuckDB to load it. Pass a file path for a persistent store, or \":memory:\" for an ephemeral one.\n\nDuckDB is columnar, so it overrides snapshot with a single native query: the read boundary for bulk/analytics workloads is one indexed scan rather than a per-entity walk. Keys and values are stored via Serialization (so any K/V work); dates are stored as integers (Dates.value). The record id comes from a DuckDB sequence. Multi-step writes (correct!, amend!, retract!) run inside a DuckDB transaction (see with_write_tx), so a crash mid-write rolls back.\n\nTwo things to note:\n\nThe on-disk format is tied to the Julia serialization version, so a database file is not guaranteed to be readable by a different major Julia version.\nKey lookups compare the serialized bytes of the key, so keys are reliable for types whose serialization is a pure function of equality (String, Symbol, integers, ...) within one serializer version. Avoid keys whose bytes can differ between two equal values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.DuckDBStore"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.MemoryStore","page":"Reference","text":"MemoryStore{K,V}()\n\nIn-memory reference backend. Records are kept per key in append order; id is (key, index) so close_tx! is an O(1) lookup. Not thread-safe; wrap in ThreadSafe for concurrent use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.MemoryStore"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.Record","page":"Reference","text":"Record{V}\n\nAppend-only bitemporal record: value over half-open [effective_from, effective_to) (effective time) and [assertive_from, assertive_to) (assertive time). Both axes are DateTime. Only assertive_to may change (see close_tx!). id is backend-assigned by put_record!.\n\nAssertive time is UTC by convention: the operations default asserted_at to now(UTC), so the \"latest assertive_from wins\" rule and audit ordering never go backwards across a DST boundary. A caller passing an explicit asserted_at is responsible for supplying UTC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.Record"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.SQLiteStore","page":"Reference","text":"SQLiteStore{K,V}(path::AbstractString; table = \"records\")\nSQLiteStore{K,V}(db::SQLite.DB; table = \"records\")\n\nSQLite-backed bitemporal store. Requires the SQLite extension: run using SQLite to load it. Pass a file path for a persistent store, or \":memory:\" for an ephemeral one.\n\nKeys and values are stored via Serialization (so any K/V work); dates are stored as integers (Dates.value). The record id is the SQLite rowid. Multi-step writes (correct!, amend!, retract!) run inside a SQLite transaction (see with_write_tx), so a crash mid-write rolls back.\n\nTwo things to note:\n\nThe on-disk format is tied to the Julia serialization version, so a database file is not guaranteed to be readable by a different major Julia version.\nKey lookups compare the serialized bytes of the key, so keys are reliable for types whose serialization is a pure function of equality (String, Symbol, integers, ...) within one serializer version. Avoid keys whose bytes can differ between two equal values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.SQLiteStore"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.ThreadSafe","page":"Reference","text":"ThreadSafe(store)\n\nWrap any BitemporalStore with a single store-wide ReentrantLock. Each operation locks once and runs its full primitive sequence atomically, so multi-primitive writes (correct!, amend!, retract!) and compound reads (as_of_batch, diff, load!) cannot interleave with a concurrent writer. This is safety, not concurrency: one operation runs at a time, store-wide.\n\nA ReentrantLock (not a SpinLock) is used deliberately: the wrapped operations may yield while holding the lock (e.g. a backend doing database IO), which a spinlock cannot do safely. On Julia 1.11+ the store is kept in a Base.Lockable, so it is only reachable while the lock is held.\n\nasof_join reads two stores and is not wrapped (locking two stores needs an ordering protocol), so a join across concurrently-written stores is not a single atomic read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.ThreadSafe"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Base.diff-Union{Tuple{BitemporalStore{K, V}}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{K}} where {K, V}","page":"Reference","text":"diff(s; assertive_at_old, assertive_at_new) -> NamedTuple of column vectors\n\nRecords whose currently-asserted value changed between two assertive times. Columns entity, effective_from, effective_to, old_value, new_value, kind, where kind is one of :inserted (present only at assertive_at_new), :retracted (present only at assertive_at_old), or :corrected (same (entity, effective_from, effective_to), different value). Unchanged rows are omitted, so the result is empty iff nothing the store believes changed. Extends Base.diff. Tables.jl-compatible.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.diff"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Base.insert!-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{BitemporalStore{K, V}, Any, Any}} where {K, V}","page":"Reference","text":"insert!(s, key, value; effective_from, effective_to = MAX_DT, asserted_at = now(UTC), check_overlap = false)\n\nRecord a new fact over [effective_from, effective_to). effective_from/effective_to accept any TimeType (a Date is taken as midnight). Returns the stored Record.\n\nBy default insert! does not check for overlap with an existing asserted record: two overlapping current assertions both stand, and a read resolves them by the as_of tie rule (latest assertive_from, then the later write). To re-state a fact, use correct!, which closes the prior assertion first. Pass check_overlap = true to instead reject an insert! that overlaps an asserted record (it points you at correct!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.insert!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.amend!-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{BitemporalStore{K, V}, Any, Any}} where {K, V}","page":"Reference","text":"amend!(s, key, value; effective, asserted_at = now(UTC)) -> Vector{Record}\n\n\"The world changed on effective.\" Close the asserted chapter(s) covering effective, re-append the old value over [effective_from, effective), and append value from effective on. Errors if nothing covers effective. Returns the newly inserted Records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.amend!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.as_of-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{BitemporalStore{K, V}, Any}} where {K, V}","page":"Reference","text":"as_of(s, key; effective_at = now(UTC), assertive_at = now(UTC)) -> Union{V,Nothing}\n\nThe value asserted at assertive_at to hold at effective_at, or nothing. Both effective_at and assertive_at accept any TimeType (a Date is taken as midnight). When more than one record is asserted over effective_at at assertive_at, the one with the latest assertive_from wins, and a tie on assertive_from resolves to the later write (append order).\n\nA record closed at the very instant it was created (assertive_to == assertive_from, e.g. a correction whose asserted_at equals an earlier write's) has an empty assertion interval: it never satisfies assertive_from <= assertive_at < assertive_to, so as_of never returns it, though history still shows it. Assertive time has millisecond resolution (DateTime), so distinct real-time writes rarely collide; load! rows sharing a key, range, and asserted_at keep only the last (the later write wins the tie).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.as_of"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.as_of_batch-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{BitemporalStore{K, V}, Vector{K}, Vector{<:Dates.TimeType}, Vector{<:Dates.TimeType}}} where {K, V}","page":"Reference","text":"as_of_batch(s, keys, effective_ats, assertive_ats; threaded = false) -> Vector{Union{V,Nothing}}\n\nVectorised as_of: position i holds the value asserted at assertive_ats[i] to hold at effective_ats[i] for keys[i], or nothing. Fetches each key's records once instead of per call.\n\nWith threaded = true the batch is split across threads (running serially when only one is available), picking a strategy from supports_parallel_reads: backends with parallel reads fetch one query per thread; the others fetch records serially (one per distinct key, connection-safe) and thread only the per-query scan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.as_of_batch"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.asof_join-Union{Tuple{Vb}, Tuple{Va}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{BitemporalStore{K, Va}, BitemporalStore{K, Vb}}} where {K, Va, Vb}","page":"Reference","text":"asof_join(a, b; effective_at = now(UTC), assertive_at = now(UTC)) -> NamedTuple of column vectors\n\nInner-join two stores on entity at one (effective_at, assertive_at) point. The stores must share the key type K. Columns entity, a, b, with one row per entity that has a value in both stores at that point (entities in only one store are dropped). Tables.jl-compatible.\n\nReads each store with its own snapshot, so wrapping the inputs in ThreadSafe makes each snapshot atomic but not the join as a whole: a join across two concurrently-written stores is not a single point-in-time read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.asof_join"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.close_tx!","page":"Reference","text":"close_tx!(s, id, asserted_at): set the record's assertive_to to asserted_at. Idempotent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.close_tx!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.correct!-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{BitemporalStore{K, V}, Any, Any}} where {K, V}","page":"Reference","text":"correct!(s, key, value; effective_from, effective_to = MAX_DT, asserted_at = now(UTC))\n\n\"We were wrong.\" Close every asserted record overlapping the range, then append the corrected value. A record only partially overlapped keeps its surrounding assertion: the slivers outside [effective_from, effective_to) are re-inserted with the old value, so correcting a subrange never silently retracts the rest. History stays readable via as_of at an earlier assertive_at. Returns the stored corrected Record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.correct!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.entities","page":"Reference","text":"entities(s): all keys the store knows about. The returned iterable must be safe to iterate after the call returns, i.e. detached from the store's internal state (return a snapshot, not a live view), so a caller behind ThreadSafe can iterate it once the lock is released.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.entities"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.get_records","page":"Reference","text":"get_records(s, key): all records for key in append order; empty if unknown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.get_records"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.history-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{BitemporalStore{K, V}, Any}} where {K, V}","page":"Reference","text":"history(s, key) -> NamedTuple of column vectors\n\nEvery record for key (including superseded), as a Tables.jl column table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.history"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.load!-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{BitemporalStore{K, V}, Any}} where {K, V}","page":"Reference","text":"load!(s, table; key, value, effective_from, asserted_at, effective_to = MAX_DT)\n\nBulk-load bitemporal observations from any Tables.jl source (a DataFrame, CSV.File, vector of NamedTuples, ...). Each mapping is a column-name Symbol, a row -> value function (for computed columns), or a constant. Rows are applied in ascending asserted_at order (ties keep source order) with the same close/sliver/insert semantics as correct!, so repeated observations of the same key and valid range chain together in assertive time. Returns s.\n\nEach key's records are fetched once and the asserted set is maintained in memory while its rows are applied, so loading N observations of one key costs one read instead of N (the per-row correct! path re-read on every row). Each key's writes run in one with_write_tx.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.load!"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.put_record!","page":"Reference","text":"put_record!(s, key, record): append record (id = nothing); return it with id assigned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.put_record!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.retract!-Union{Tuple{V}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{BitemporalStore{K, V}, Any}} where {K, V}","page":"Reference","text":"retract!(s, key; effective_from, effective_to = MAX_DT, asserted_at = now(UTC)) -> Vector{Record}\n\n\"There is no value here anymore.\" Close every asserted record overlapping [effective_from, effective_to) without inserting a replacement, so as_of over the range returns nothing at assertive_at >= asserted_at while the prior assertion stays reproducible at an earlier assertive_at. A partially-overlapped record keeps its surrounding slivers, exactly as correct!. Returns the re-inserted sliver records (empty for a full retraction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.retract!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.snapshot-Union{Tuple{BitemporalStore{K, V}}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{K}} where {K, V}","page":"Reference","text":"snapshot(s; effective_at = nothing, assertive_at = now(UTC)) -> NamedTuple of column vectors\n\nColumnar point-in-time view of the whole store, the read boundary for bulk workloads, since freezing assertive_at is reproducible and leakage-proof. With effective_at = nothing, one row per record asserted at assertive_at, columns entity, value, effective_from, effective_to. With a effective_at (any TimeType), the collapsed cross-section: columns entity, value, one row per entity that has a value there. Tables.jl-compatible.\n\nRow order is backend-defined: a backend with a native snapshot (e.g. DuckDBStore) may return rows in a different order than the generic path. Do not rely on it; sort or index by entity if you need a stable order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.snapshot"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.supports_parallel_reads-Tuple{BitemporalStore}","page":"Reference","text":"supports_parallel_reads(s) -> Bool\n\nWhether get_records on s is cheap and safe to call from many threads at once. In-memory backends (MemoryStore, ColumnarStore) return true; backends over a single mutable connection (SQLiteStore, DuckDBStore) keep the false default. This selects the strategy as_of_batch(...; threaded = true) uses, so a new backend only overrides it when concurrent get_records is safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.supports_parallel_reads"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#BiTemporalData.with_write_tx-Tuple{Any, BitemporalStore}","page":"Reference","text":"with_write_tx(f, s)\n\nRun f() atomically on s if the backend supports transactions, otherwise just f(). Multi-step writes (correct!, amend!, retract!) wrap their body in this so a crash midway cannot leave a transactional backend half-updated. The default is a no-op (f()); transactional backends override it. Returns f()'s value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BiTemporalData.with_write_tx"},{"category":"section","location":"#BiTemporalData","page":"BiTemporalData","text":"BiTemporalData stores facts along two independent time axes:\n\neffective time: when a fact is true in the world (effective_from, effective_to);\nassertive time: when the system asserted it (assertive_from, assertive_to).\n\nTracking both lets you separate the world changed from we changed our mind, and reproduce exactly what was known at any past point in time. All intervals are half-open [from, to), and writes are append-only; the only mutation is closing a record's assertive_to.\n\nThe examples below run during the docs build and share one store s. Timestamps are passed explicitly (asserted_at =) for reproducibility; real callers omit them and get now().","title":"BiTemporalData"},{"category":"section","location":"#Recording-a-fact","page":"BiTemporalData","text":"using BiTemporalData, Dates\n\n# String-keyed store of Float64 values.\ns = MemoryStore{String, Float64}()\n\ninsert!(s, \"AAPL\", 100.0; effective_from = Date(2024, 1, 1), asserted_at = DateTime(2024, 1, 1))\n\nas_of(s, \"AAPL\"; effective_at = Date(2024, 6, 1), assertive_at = DateTime(2024, 1, 2))","title":"Recording a fact"},{"category":"section","location":"#Correcting-a-mistake","page":"BiTemporalData","text":"correct! supersedes a value we now assert was wrong. The old record is not deleted; it is closed in assertive time and stays readable:\n\ncorrect!(s, \"AAPL\", 110.0; effective_from = Date(2024, 1, 1), asserted_at = DateTime(2024, 1, 3))\n\n(\n    believed_before = as_of(s, \"AAPL\"; effective_at = Date(2024, 6, 1), assertive_at = DateTime(2024, 1, 2)),\n    believed_after = as_of(s, \"AAPL\"; effective_at = Date(2024, 6, 1), assertive_at = DateTime(2024, 1, 5)),\n)","title":"Correcting a mistake"},{"category":"section","location":"#Amending-when-the-world-changes","page":"BiTemporalData","text":"amend! is different: the past value was right, but the world changed on a date. It splits the timeline, keeping the old value before the change and the new value after:\n\namend!(s, \"AAPL\", 130.0; effective = Date(2024, 7, 1), asserted_at = DateTime(2024, 8, 1))\n\n(\n    spring = as_of(s, \"AAPL\"; effective_at = Date(2024, 3, 1), assertive_at = DateTime(2024, 8, 2)),\n    autumn = as_of(s, \"AAPL\"; effective_at = Date(2024, 9, 1), assertive_at = DateTime(2024, 8, 2)),\n)","title":"Amending when the world changes"},{"category":"section","location":"#History-and-snapshots","page":"BiTemporalData","text":"history returns every record ever written for a key, superseded ones included: the full audit trail.\n\nhistory(s, \"AAPL\")\n\nsnapshot is the read boundary for bulk/analytics workloads: a flat, columnar view frozen at a assertive_at, which makes it reproducible and leakage-proof. With a effective_at it collapses to one value per entity:\n\nsnapshot(s; effective_at = Date(2024, 9, 1), assertive_at = DateTime(2024, 8, 2))","title":"History and snapshots"},{"category":"section","location":"#Analytical-queries","page":"BiTemporalData","text":"Three operations build on snapshot. diff reports what the store's assertions changed between two assertive times, classifying each row as :inserted, :retracted, or :corrected:\n\ndiff(s; assertive_at_old = DateTime(2024, 1, 2), assertive_at_new = DateTime(2024, 8, 2))\n\nas_of_batch answers many point-in-time lookups in one pass, ideal for building leakage-free training sets:\n\nas_of_batch(\n    s,\n    [\"AAPL\", \"AAPL\"],\n    [Date(2024, 3, 1), Date(2024, 9, 1)],\n    [DateTime(2024, 8, 2), DateTime(2024, 8, 2)],\n)\n\nasof_join inner-joins two stores on entity at one point in time.\n\nSee the Reference for the full API.","title":"Analytical queries"}]
}
